&reftitle.description;
&style.oop;
public DateTimeDateTime::setTime inthour intminute intsecond0 intmicrosecond0
&style.procedural;
DateTimedate_time_set DateTimeobject inthour intminute intsecond0 intmicrosecond0
Resets the current time of the DateTime object to a different time.
Like DateTimeImmutable::setTime but works with DateTime.
The procedural version takes the DateTime object as its first argument.

  &reftitle.examples;

   DateTime::setTime example
   &style.oop;


<?php
$date = new DateTime('2001-01-01');

$date->setTime(14, 55);
echo $date->format('Y-m-d H:i:s') . "\n";

$date->setTime(14, 55, 24);
echo $date->format('Y-m-d H:i:s') . "\n";


   &example.outputs;


2001-01-01 14:55:00
2001-01-01 14:55:24


   &style.procedural;


<?php
$date = date_create('2001-01-01');

date_time_set($date, 14, 55);
echo date_format($date, 'Y-m-d H:i:s') . "\n";

date_time_set($date, 14, 55, 24);
echo date_format($date, 'Y-m-d H:i:s') . "\n";


   &example.outputs;


2001-01-01 14:55:00
2001-01-01 14:55:24

&reftitle.changelog;





    &Version;
    &Description;





    8.1.0
    The behaviour with double existing hours (during the fall-back
    DST transition) changed. Previously PHP would pick the second occurrence
    (after the DST transition), instead of the first occurrence (before DST
     transition).



    7.1.0
    The microsecond parameter was added.
